1
00:00:01,240 --> 00:00:05,877
In this video, we'll do
an Introduction to Device Drivers.

2
00:00:05,877 --> 00:00:09,680
So the learning objectives, we're going to
understand the role of device drivers.

3
00:00:09,680 --> 00:00:13,370
We're going to talk a little bit
about device driver design, and

4
00:00:13,370 --> 00:00:15,394
we're going to introduce modules.

5
00:00:15,394 --> 00:00:19,576
So starting off with the structure
of operating systems and

6
00:00:19,576 --> 00:00:23,140
some common operating systems structures.

7
00:00:23,140 --> 00:00:28,027
Talking about how this interaction
works between the system or user space,

8
00:00:28,027 --> 00:00:30,701
application and the operating system and

9
00:00:30,701 --> 00:00:34,226
how that varies based on
the operating system design.

10
00:00:34,226 --> 00:00:38,918
So there's three basic designs that
you can have for an operating system.

11
00:00:38,918 --> 00:00:44,056
One would be monolithic where
you have the the entire

12
00:00:44,056 --> 00:00:51,545
content of the kernel running in
a protective mode in the operating system.

13
00:00:51,545 --> 00:00:55,351
And your all applications
then live in user space.

14
00:00:56,710 --> 00:01:00,896
The second is a micro kernel type
of set up where you have some

15
00:01:00,896 --> 00:01:04,581
applications running in
the system user space, and

16
00:01:04,581 --> 00:01:08,951
then you have user mode extending
into the operating system.

17
00:01:08,951 --> 00:01:13,922
And so there's a set of kernel
capabilities that are run at

18
00:01:13,922 --> 00:01:16,163
higher privilege level.

19
00:01:16,163 --> 00:01:18,700
But there's also a set of operating system

20
00:01:18,700 --> 00:01:21,456
capabilities that
are served from user mode.

21
00:01:21,456 --> 00:01:27,544
And so
an example of micro kernel is FreeRTOS.

22
00:01:27,544 --> 00:01:30,656
And then there's also the hybrid
kernel where there is some combination

23
00:01:30,656 --> 00:01:31,173
of the two.

24
00:01:31,173 --> 00:01:36,700
That there's some basic functionality
that's in kernel mode, there's also

25
00:01:36,700 --> 00:01:42,160
some device drivers and other capability
that is implemented in kernel mode.

26
00:01:42,160 --> 00:01:47,144
But there's also pieces that
are provided by the operating system

27
00:01:47,144 --> 00:01:49,822
that are implemented in user mode.

28
00:01:49,822 --> 00:01:54,130
So for this class, what we're talking
about is the monolithic kernel-based

29
00:01:54,130 --> 00:01:56,492
operating system that we have with Linux.

30
00:01:56,492 --> 00:02:01,881
Where everything that's running
in as a part of the operating

31
00:02:01,881 --> 00:02:07,592
system is running in kernel mode
at that higher privilege level.

32
00:02:07,592 --> 00:02:10,943
So what do we mean when we
talk about a device driver?

33
00:02:10,943 --> 00:02:15,741
The goal or the function of the device
driver is to make a hardware respond to

34
00:02:15,741 --> 00:02:18,343
a well defined abstraction interface.

35
00:02:18,343 --> 00:02:23,110
And so all those system calls that
we talked about in the first part of

36
00:02:23,110 --> 00:02:27,878
this course, we're going to take
some subset of those system calls,

37
00:02:27,878 --> 00:02:30,710
read write, for instance.

38
00:02:30,710 --> 00:02:36,023
And we're going to map those into
functions that interact with the hardware.

39
00:02:36,023 --> 00:02:39,640
And that's really going to be
the purpose of the device driver.

40
00:02:39,640 --> 00:02:42,570
The device driver can be built
separately from the kernel and

41
00:02:42,570 --> 00:02:45,230
it could be added dynamically
whenever it's needed.

42
00:02:45,230 --> 00:02:48,818
So this is useful because if
the device is not a part of the system,

43
00:02:48,818 --> 00:02:51,641
there would be no reason
to load it into the kernel.

44
00:02:54,660 --> 00:02:59,447
The book also talks about mechanism
versus policy that a driver should

45
00:02:59,447 --> 00:03:01,990
provide mechanism and not policy.

46
00:03:01,990 --> 00:03:05,517
And when we talk about mechanisms,
what we mean is that

47
00:03:05,517 --> 00:03:09,593
we're providing access to
the capabilities of the hardware.

48
00:03:09,593 --> 00:03:14,339
And when we talk about policy, we're
talking about how would you use those

49
00:03:14,339 --> 00:03:16,911
capabilities for a specific instance?

50
00:03:16,911 --> 00:03:22,246
So some examples of the book gives
a TCP/IP socket as an example

51
00:03:22,246 --> 00:03:27,278
of a capability and a policy and
how it would actually be used

52
00:03:27,278 --> 00:03:32,440
would be specific services or
specific serving of content.

53
00:03:32,440 --> 00:03:36,764
Similarly for hard disk drive,
the blocks of a hard disk drive,

54
00:03:36,764 --> 00:03:41,891
it would be a driver that would provide
the mechanism to access the blocks and

55
00:03:41,891 --> 00:03:44,640
store binary data on blocks of the disk.

56
00:03:44,640 --> 00:03:48,014
And it would be a file
system that would provide

57
00:03:48,014 --> 00:03:52,002
the policy of how you can
use those particular blocks.

58
00:03:52,002 --> 00:03:56,904
So the driver that's driving
the hardware would be responsible for

59
00:03:56,904 --> 00:04:02,677
the capability and the filesystem driver
would be responsible for the policy.

60
00:04:05,741 --> 00:04:10,278
So in order to write a policy
free driver for hardware,

61
00:04:10,278 --> 00:04:14,718
what you want to do is basically
make the least amount of

62
00:04:14,718 --> 00:04:19,375
restrictions as possible on
a user space application.

63
00:04:19,375 --> 00:04:23,311
Or another driver that's that's
attempting to use your hardware device.

64
00:04:24,540 --> 00:04:28,257
So that means that you want to
support both synchronous and

65
00:04:28,257 --> 00:04:32,601
asynchronous operation,
be able to open multiple times at once.

66
00:04:32,601 --> 00:04:37,216
You want to allow multiple user
space applications to access your

67
00:04:37,216 --> 00:04:39,017
device at the same time.

68
00:04:39,017 --> 00:04:43,110
The book warns against adding additional
layers to try to simplify things.

69
00:04:43,110 --> 00:04:48,063
A lot of times that basically
turns into enforcing policy

70
00:04:48,063 --> 00:04:50,760
on how your device can be used.

71
00:04:50,760 --> 00:04:56,606
And what the book recommends is
if you want to give an example

72
00:04:56,606 --> 00:05:01,512
about how you can simplify
things in user space.

73
00:05:01,512 --> 00:05:06,837
A lot of times you'll bundle your
driver with application that

74
00:05:06,837 --> 00:05:12,371
runs in user space that can give
you an example about how to use it.

75
00:05:12,371 --> 00:05:16,541
So some of the roles that are going to
be provided for us by the kernel, and

76
00:05:16,541 --> 00:05:19,723
we've talked about these
already in the first part of

77
00:05:19,723 --> 00:05:23,850
this course from the perspective of
writing user space code as well.

78
00:05:23,850 --> 00:05:28,582
So the process management is
going to provide for us creating and

79
00:05:28,582 --> 00:05:33,405
destroying processes,
handling the I/O for processes, and

80
00:05:33,405 --> 00:05:37,420
scheduling processes to be run or
stop or replaced.

81
00:05:37,420 --> 00:05:38,990
And also memory management.

82
00:05:38,990 --> 00:05:41,837
So that virtual address space for
processes,

83
00:05:41,837 --> 00:05:44,770
that's all going to be
controlled by the kernel.

84
00:05:44,770 --> 00:05:49,410
And when we call malloc and free from user
space, kernel is going to be responsible

85
00:05:49,410 --> 00:05:53,861
for either allocating or freeing
the memory that's associated with that.

86
00:05:53,861 --> 00:05:58,744
The everything is a file
paradigm that we've talked about,

87
00:05:58,744 --> 00:06:05,077
the place where we will locate our device
nodes will be part of the file system.

88
00:06:05,077 --> 00:06:07,712
And then all the rest of the files
that you would interact with,

89
00:06:07,712 --> 00:06:10,570
the normal files are all going to be
located in the file system as well.

90
00:06:10,570 --> 00:06:15,317
There are lots of different
file systems supported, ext4,

91
00:06:15,317 --> 00:06:18,339
fat, NTFS are just a handful of those.

92
00:06:18,339 --> 00:06:22,565
And then device control is also a part
of what the kernel is going to provide.

93
00:06:22,565 --> 00:06:25,264
So the endpoint of system operations,

94
00:06:25,264 --> 00:06:30,008
meaning the way that you would
interact with the device through those

95
00:06:30,008 --> 00:06:34,115
read write Actel type of methods
that I mentioned earlier.

96
00:06:34,115 --> 00:06:39,724
And there's going to be a device driver
for every active device in the system

97
00:06:39,724 --> 00:06:45,810
that will essentially provide
the functionality for that endpoint.

98
00:06:45,810 --> 00:06:48,830
The other kernel role is networking.

99
00:06:48,830 --> 00:06:52,647
So collecting, identifying,
dispatching network packets,

100
00:06:52,647 --> 00:06:56,394
routing address resolution,
everything that gets us into and

101
00:06:56,394 --> 00:07:00,721
out of the network is happening because
of functionality in the kernel.

102
00:07:00,721 --> 00:07:04,114
So what's happening from user space,
that's really where all the applications

103
00:07:04,114 --> 00:07:07,380
are, everything that we've talked about
from the beginning of this course.

104
00:07:07,380 --> 00:07:12,232
So if you're thinking about an FTP server,
for example, that wouldn't be implemented

105
00:07:12,232 --> 00:07:15,360
in the kernel,
that would be implemented in user space.

106
00:07:15,360 --> 00:07:17,679
Same with the graphics session manager,

107
00:07:17,679 --> 00:07:21,531
that would not be something that
would be implemented in the kernel.

108
00:07:21,531 --> 00:07:24,723
That's an application that will
be implemented in user space.

109
00:07:24,723 --> 00:07:30,583
And then as I mentioned before, the policy
around access of device drivers or

110
00:07:30,583 --> 00:07:33,829
examples about how to use device drivers,

111
00:07:33,829 --> 00:07:38,972
those utility applications that
would interact with your driver,

112
00:07:38,972 --> 00:07:42,691
those would all be running
in user space as well.

113
00:07:42,691 --> 00:07:47,605
So we talked about drivers being kernel
code that's controlling hardware

114
00:07:47,605 --> 00:07:50,186
that may or may not be added at runtime.

115
00:07:50,186 --> 00:07:53,852
When we're talking about
adding driver at runtime,

116
00:07:53,852 --> 00:07:57,370
what we're talking about
in that case is a module.

117
00:07:57,370 --> 00:08:02,970
And so module is just another name for
a kernel code that's added at runtime.

118
00:08:02,970 --> 00:08:06,557
And often this is used for
device drivers and it's useful for

119
00:08:06,557 --> 00:08:10,567
device drivers because your system
may not have specific devices,

120
00:08:10,567 --> 00:08:14,034
in that case there's no reason
to load it into the kernel.

121
00:08:14,034 --> 00:08:18,800
So you can use the dynamic
features to only load the kernel

122
00:08:18,800 --> 00:08:21,688
modules that you need at runtime.

123
00:08:21,688 --> 00:08:27,150
There are several user space utilities
that are used to add modules to a running

124
00:08:27,150 --> 00:08:32,545
kernel, insmod is one that would link
a module file into the running kernel.

125
00:08:32,545 --> 00:08:36,102
Rmmod would remove the file
from the running kernel, and

126
00:08:36,102 --> 00:08:39,807
modprobe is another one that
works similar to insmod in that

127
00:08:39,807 --> 00:08:43,087
it can link a kernel module
into the running kernel.

128
00:08:43,087 --> 00:08:47,789
But it will also include dependencies,
and so if your driver references

129
00:08:47,789 --> 00:08:52,566
other drivers, for instance, modprobe
can track that and it can load any

130
00:08:52,566 --> 00:08:57,595
dependencies that you have in addition
to driver that you've asked to load.

131
00:08:57,595 --> 00:09:01,753
So the book also shows this
split view of the kernel.

132
00:09:01,753 --> 00:09:04,190
All the things that the kernel
is responsible for.

133
00:09:04,190 --> 00:09:08,367
We've talked about the system call
interface being the the interface into

134
00:09:08,367 --> 00:09:09,110
the kernel.

135
00:09:09,110 --> 00:09:12,283
And what you'll notice
here is this small box

136
00:09:12,283 --> 00:09:17,230
is listed as the features that
are typically implemented as modules.

137
00:09:17,230 --> 00:09:21,230
And so you see a lot of these
lined up here along the bottom

138
00:09:21,230 --> 00:09:23,971
that are interacting with hardware.

139
00:09:23,971 --> 00:09:28,527
Because as I mentioned, the fact that
you're interacting with hardware means

140
00:09:28,527 --> 00:09:32,010
that the hardware might be
different on different systems.

141
00:09:32,010 --> 00:09:35,140
So these are cases the block
character devices and

142
00:09:35,140 --> 00:09:40,286
interface drivers since there's going to
be interacting with specific hardware,

143
00:09:40,286 --> 00:09:42,832
they are often implemented as modules.

144
00:09:42,832 --> 00:09:46,831
So that they can be dynamically loaded at
runtime to match whatever hardware you're

145
00:09:46,831 --> 00:09:47,540
running on.

146
00:09:47,540 --> 00:09:51,884
And filesystem types are another case
where these are optionally loaded

147
00:09:51,884 --> 00:09:56,811
at runtime, depending on which file system
you happen to be using on your device.